1
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:18,310
 Another day begins inside Jumbo Jr.'s body, and for the components of his bloodstream, it's rush hour as usual.

2
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:23,330
 The red corpuscles are too busy to stop and watch those idle platelets dance.

3
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,360
 Why on earth can't you ever keep your great-sons ten feet to yourself?

4
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:33,060
 They don't like being out in this heavy traffic. Why couldn't they all have stayed at home and turned on the TV instead?

5
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,400
 Look at all the fat. He's getting into bad habits again.

6
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:43,100
 Now there's our old friend Professor Globus on the left, with Hemo in the middle and Little Globin on the right.

7
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:49,260
 They've got a long road to travel today, and there are a few surprises up ahead for our corpuscle friends.

8
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:57,810
 What on earth is it?

9
00:00:58,130 --> 00:00:59,520
 What is this horrid thing?

10
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:04,120
 It's rubbish, actually. A broken piece of amino acid that's no use to anyone.

11
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:12,510
 So won't you please explain why it is that the blood vessels are so awfully full of rubbish all the time?

12
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,120
 Well, the bloodstream, as you know, reaches every cell in the human body.

13
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:21,280
 It delivers vital supplies like oxygen, minerals, protein, fats, and vitamins.

14
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:31,680
 Oh yes, and look at that. Don't forget the hormones, children, and the blood also carries away the waste products from the cells to be converted by the liver or filtered out by the kidneys.

15
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:36,660
 And since we're getting pretty near the kidneys, there's more and more rubbish floating about.

16
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:39,560
 See what I mean?

17
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:45,450
 It won't get you there any more quickly.

18
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,620
 Before you start merging with the traffic, just remember who has the right of way.

19
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:54,030
 Make way, make way, please move aside, thank you.

20
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:57,880
 Urgent delivery of protein. Thank you. Please move aside.

21
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,260
 You sugars had better go and report to the liver at once.

22
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:08,590
 I've caught you in the act, cholesterol. Move along there. I'll have no blockages here.

23
00:02:08,910 --> 00:02:11,510
 And what about you? Have you proof of identity?

24
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:17,720
 Woman, officer, adrenaline's my name. Please, could you possibly tell me what the most direct route to the heart is?

25
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,760
 Any vein will take you there in the end.

26
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:23,660
 Mind you, I suppose it's true that some routes are more direct than others.

27
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:28,560
 Go straight up here for two centimeters, then take the fork to the left and you'll find yourself in an archery.

28
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,660
 No, I mean a vein. You can't miss it.

29
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:33,320
 Then you'll find another policeman and ask him the way.

30
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:37,900
 Possibly give me a push start. My engine seems to have stalled when I landed here.

31
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:45,150
 Thanks all the same.

32
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:48,420
 You, and where are your papers?

33
00:02:49,710 --> 00:02:51,980
 My papers, did you say, officer?

34
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:54,800
 Oh, silly me. I've gone and left them at home.

35
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,220
 Would you like me to run back and fetch them for you? I'd be there and back in a minute.

36
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:03,420
 I quite understand, sir. It's very easy to forget your identity card when you're in a hurry.

37
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:06,060
 Luckily, I won't need to see your papers.

38
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:14,330
 Can't beat that for Chica. Virus in the main vein just marching along as if he owned the place.

39
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:15,820
 And who are you?

40
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:21,710
 We are law-abiding red corpuscles proceeding to the kidneys on business subject to your approval, officer.

41
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:30,990
 Disgusting.

42
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:40,890
 Well, these little horrors, I don't like the look of them one bit.

43
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:42,510
 You can say that again, Lieutenant.

44
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:45,370
 These girls are pure poison, ammonia molecules.

45
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:47,870
 They're on their way to a reformatory in the liver.

46
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:50,750
 They're hoping to turn them into young ladies.

47
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:52,990
 Hey, you, keep away from that door.

48
00:03:53,230 --> 00:03:56,330
 You know very well you're not allowed to get off this vehicle until we arrive in the liver.

49
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:00,500
 Back for just one minute and they take advantage.

50
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,270
 The sooner I can get rid of them, the better.

51
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:19,230
 Here we are in the liver.

52
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:23,060
 This will be your new home and I know you're going to love the school.

53
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:42,020
 Shout at me, I'm only carrying out my orders.

54
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:45,980
 and according to these orders, I must deliver these girls to reform school in the liver.

55
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:48,510
 And as far as I'm concerned, that is that.

56
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:50,000
 Chock-a-block.

57
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:52,320
 In fact, things are getting rapidly out of hand.

58
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:06,880
 Spirit, buy the cell next door. You might have better luck there.

59
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:15,800
 Plan to upload your little horrors on me?

60
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:17,890
 I can take a hint. Bye.

61
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:35,340
 I have some wayward girls who need to be taught the error of their ways.

62
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:38,260
 Tell me the sob story. I know what those girls are like.

63
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:42,900
 But we do have a few vacancies, so you'd better send them in before I lose my nerve.

64
00:05:42,900 --> 00:06:29,510
 Yes, your behavior and be punished because I will have no other option but to expel you from this school.

65
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:31,610
 He can't really chuck us out, can he?

66
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:36,090
 Yes, he can't. We're far too poisonous to let loose. He's only trying to put the wind up us.

67
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:39,250
 Oh, really? Now watch me while I put the wind up him.

68
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:56,210
 Poisonous, and before the body can deal with it, it must be converted into urea.

69
00:06:57,330 --> 00:06:58,910
 Listen already.

70
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:03,100
 Who's in the classroom?

71
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:09,200
 Now repeat after me. We'll have less poison, never fear, and better manners as urea.

72
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:16,810
 I don't really think very much of your sense of humor. Now you say it.

73
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:30,940
 Unexpected pleasure and privilege to be inspected by you, sir.

74
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:37,720
 Visit from the chief inspector of schools is something we all look forward to, sir.

75
00:07:38,030 --> 00:07:41,210
 He's coming along the corridor. You've got four seconds to pull your socks.

76
00:07:55,270 --> 00:07:58,490
 Gratifying to see such a well-behaved class. Good morning, girls.

77
00:07:58,730 --> 00:07:59,490
 Good morning.

78
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:05,330
 Good morning, girls. Do you remember when you first came to this school?

79
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:09,810
 You were ammonia monocles in those days. Not very nice to know, I'm afraid.

80
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:11,700
 Downright poisonous, in fact.

81
00:08:11,930 --> 00:08:17,860
 Now that you've acquired an education and are ready to graduate, I've brought along your diplomas.

82
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:20,400
 Please queue up so that I can hand them over.

83
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:28,150
 First in line, please step forward.

84
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:32,660
 By wearing this, you're free to travel about the organism.

85
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:34,020
 And here's your diploma.

86
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:35,340
 Next.

87
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:41,540
 Passing the great chemical works of the liver, and they pump out a lot of pollution.

88
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:45,360
 I'm afraid there's nothing else for it but to put up with this situation.

89
00:09:56,220 --> 00:10:07,070
 To interfere.

90
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:13,970
 This, that stuff feels absolutely a faulty.

91
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,250
 I'm sick.

92
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:18,990
 What is the smell, prof?

93
00:10:18,990 --> 00:10:22,710
 It's urea, one of the main waste products the body has to get rid of.

94
00:10:23,010 --> 00:10:26,110
 We'll have to live with it until we're ready for the big clean-up.

95
00:10:26,170 --> 00:10:30,450
 When we get to the kidneys, here, try one of these, it helps a bit.

96
00:10:46,910 --> 00:10:49,290
 To put over my knee and your...

97
00:10:49,290 --> 00:10:50,890
 Girls will be girls, you know.

98
00:10:51,930 --> 00:10:54,130
 It's not far to the kidneys now, children.

99
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:56,140
 The big clean-up, you wait and see.

100
00:10:56,770 --> 00:10:59,390
 Kidneys are fascinating organs, they are filters, actually.

101
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:04,040
 Kidney is a purification plant of amazing efficiency.

102
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:06,800
 It's a pity about the one small drawback, though.

103
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:09,100
 One small drawback, prof? What's the problem?

104
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:17,570
 Here's the drawback. The kidneys can purify only one liter of blood per minute. That's their total capacity.

105
00:11:17,570 --> 00:11:21,020
 Prof, why is that bad? A liter a minute sounds like enough to me.

106
00:11:21,020 --> 00:11:25,980
 Par from it, Hemo, because the heart pumps blood at a rate of five liters per minute, you see.

107
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:29,140
 Why, that must mean that four liters of the blood never gets cleaned.

108
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:30,400
 We can't stay dirty forever.

109
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,600
 Don't get carried away, Hemo. All the blood gets cleaned one-fifth at a time.

110
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:39,330
 Look, there's the renal artery on the right. It goes straight to a kidney. Come on.

111
00:11:39,330 --> 00:11:50,330
 Hey, indicate next time.

112
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:56,930
 Hey, just where do you corpuscles think you're going? Didn't you leave the left lane?

113
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:57,970
 Not at all, officer.

114
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:00,590
 This route leads to the kidney.

115
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:02,630
 Not kidding.

116
00:12:02,710 --> 00:12:04,570
 Look here, are you trying to take the mickey out of me?

117
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:07,740
 There's a great deal of steak in the kidney, officer.

118
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:11,340
 Yes, we want to swim and get clean and grab behind our ears.

119
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:13,360
 Quickly then, off you go while I'm not watching.

120
00:12:16,590 --> 00:12:25,940
 Friends march off into one of the kidneys, a filtration plant which removes impurities from the blood.

121
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:32,130
 Densely packed blood vessels are closely interwoven with tiny knotted tubes.

122
00:12:32,130 --> 00:12:37,170
 the impurities, then let them flow away in successively larger tubes.

123
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:44,580
 Operating perfectly. What a design.

124
00:12:44,840 --> 00:12:46,980
 Come here, you two, and see how it works.

125
00:12:48,010 --> 00:12:49,850
 That's how the blood flows in and out.

126
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:54,870
 What happens to the blood in between these two points is absolutely astounding.

127
00:12:55,770 --> 00:12:57,610
 All the waste products are removed,

128
00:12:57,950 --> 00:13:01,320
 and poisonous substances are eliminated from the circulation.

129
00:13:02,070 --> 00:13:04,330
 It is here that urine is manufactured.

130
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:06,130
 The glomerulus.

131
00:13:06,130 --> 00:13:09,820
 It's an amazing installation, my friend.

132
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:12,120
 What a processing plant.

133
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:18,020
 And each kidney has a million of these separators in constant operation.

134
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:21,740
 A million? Can you believe it?

135
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:26,030
 Now, children, pay attention. We're nearly at the roller coaster.

136
00:13:26,330 --> 00:13:28,990
 So the kidney's a funfair complete with roller coaster?

137
00:13:29,330 --> 00:13:32,850
 No, not really, but you'll have just as much fun in the Bowman's capsule.

138
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:33,950
 The Bowman's capsule?

139
00:13:34,410 --> 00:13:35,190
 Wait and see.

140
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:51,610
 The kit entitles you to one turn. You can't go round twice.

141
00:13:51,870 --> 00:13:53,110
 Now remember to hang on tight.

142
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:58,810
 One of these things before, will I get dizzy and fall off when I get sick?

143
00:13:58,990 --> 00:14:02,430
 Don't worry, I'll be there beside you and you can hold my hand.

144
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:06,590
 Well, this is the big clean-up at last. Afterwards, you'll come out feeling wonderful.

145
00:14:06,710 --> 00:14:08,850
 But that's afterwards, Professor. What about during?

146
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:14,970
 If it were that awful, my child, you wouldn't find an old corpuscle like me so keen to repeat the experience.

147
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:16,510
 So come on, let's go.

148
00:14:16,570 --> 00:14:19,310
 Don't take all day. Some of us are in a hurry, you know.

149
00:14:19,670 --> 00:14:20,620
 Take it away.

150
00:15:22,650 --> 00:16:14,960
 Don't even think about it.

151
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:19,020
 To get back to the start of the rollercoaster again, you have to circulate around the entire body.

152
00:16:19,310 --> 00:16:21,130
 So let's get circulating, shall we?

153
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:23,640
 Me and my clever ideas.

154
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:24,620
 You said it.

155
00:16:25,730 --> 00:16:30,240
 Well, the first stage of the filtration process is complete. Do you think we should wake him up?

156
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:31,480
 I wouldn't bother.

157
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:36,200
 As you can see, he's far too busy to be disturbed. He's thinking.

158
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:38,450
 In any case, the kidney runs automatically.

159
00:16:46,730 --> 00:16:53,380
 It's a real pick-me-up, isn't it?

160
00:16:53,620 --> 00:16:54,760
 Kidneys are amazing.

161
00:16:54,760 --> 00:17:00,080
 Oh, yes, we may be finished for now, but there are other components of the bloodstream which still have to be dealt with.

162
00:17:00,370 --> 00:17:02,770
 Everything in the blood must be filtered and purified.

163
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:09,920
 99% of the material is restored to the body after separation and purification.

164
00:17:11,310 --> 00:17:15,440
 It leaves only a teeny 1% to be gotten rid off.

165
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:20,200
 Professor, the kidney is fascinating, but where do we go from here?

166
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:24,450
 We're off to the heart first of all, then on to the lungs to pick up some oxygen.

167
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:26,820
 How does that idea appeal to you, Hemo?

168
00:17:26,940 --> 00:17:27,660
 I love it.

169
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:48,490
 Mr. Mate, keep her steady as she goes.

170
00:17:49,270 --> 00:17:51,810
 Pull the stern and stop.

171
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:52,500
 Drop anchor.

172
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:55,520
 Blood needs water.

173
00:17:56,050 --> 00:17:57,710
 Recover the water removed by filtration.

174
00:17:58,550 --> 00:18:01,470
 New stocks of salt urgently needed to meet shortage.

175
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:03,120
 Aye, aye, sir.

176
00:18:03,990 --> 00:18:06,060
 Sugar stocks are running low.

177
00:18:06,610 --> 00:18:07,590
 Save as much as you can.

178
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:11,240
 Get the water-gathering hormones over the side now.

179
00:18:12,070 --> 00:18:14,050
 And some L-L-thus.

180
00:18:14,570 --> 00:18:17,630
 Ah, aldosterone hormones to collect salt.

181
00:18:27,130 --> 00:18:35,880
 Be hearties, let's get that water on board.

182
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:36,760
 All away.

183
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:39,740
 You can stir those droplets in the starboard hole.

184
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:43,330
 They'll start loading salt now.

185
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:52,300
 Throw it onto the port hatch.

186
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:53,460
 Lower away, easy.

187
00:18:56,520 --> 00:19:01,580
 Wish I'd start biting.

188
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:03,580
 Must you always be so impatient?

189
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:07,220
 Only yesterday I caught one this size, I'm telling you.

190
00:19:07,470 --> 00:19:10,020
 I just wish you fellas had been there to see the size of it.

191
00:19:10,060 --> 00:19:13,060
 It was an unbelievable length, really it was, I swear.

192
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:15,940
 Aye, it must have weighed at least 400 kilojoules.

193
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:18,600
 Come off it, we've heard your stories before.

194
00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:20,340
 I never exaggerate.

195
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:23,500
 For example, take that monster I caught yesterday.

196
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:26,940
 This one's a whale, this one's a monster.

197
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:29,380
 That'll teach you to disbelieve me, won't it?

198
00:19:29,630 --> 00:19:32,150
 Just you wait till I land him, boys.

199
00:19:32,590 --> 00:19:36,380
 It's unfair to the rest of us. Why does Jock always get the best places?

200
00:19:36,910 --> 00:19:39,090
 I insist on swapping with you right now!

201
00:19:39,410 --> 00:19:41,090
 The whale of the catch, all right?

202
00:19:43,130 --> 00:19:46,300
 That one bigger than yesterday's, hmm?

203
00:19:46,470 --> 00:19:51,370
 By no means. In fact, now that I come to think of it, it was enormous compared to this midget.

204
00:19:51,690 --> 00:19:53,850
 Aye, it must have been all of 500 kilojoules.

205
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:55,850
 Maybe even 750.

206
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:10,620
 Captain, there is a man overboard!

207
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:12,390
 What was that you said?

208
00:20:12,390 --> 00:20:13,990
 I said man overboard.

209
00:20:14,670 --> 00:20:15,070
 Louder.

210
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:17,960
 There's a man. Actually, he's a corpuscle.

211
00:20:18,230 --> 00:20:20,430
 And he's drowning in front of your very nose.

212
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:26,230
 I see him. He's a red corpuscle, all right.

213
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:28,010
 What's he doing here, of all places?

214
00:20:28,380 --> 00:20:29,750
 It's against the rules.

215
00:20:30,320 --> 00:20:32,830
 We can't have red corpuscles floating in the urine.

216
00:20:33,490 --> 00:20:35,590
 He must be rescued as soon as humanly possible.

217
00:20:35,730 --> 00:20:36,290
 Aye, aye, Captain.

218
00:20:40,490 --> 00:20:49,800
 Get up on deck and find out why they haven't rescued that poor corpuscle yet.

219
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:52,660
 Throw him a life belt, you landlubber, you.

220
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:05,360
 Tights at all, guys. You can pull him in now.

221
00:21:06,870 --> 00:21:10,590
 Done, lads. You're safe at last.

222
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:12,730
 But how does a corpuscle end up in the soup?

223
00:21:12,770 --> 00:21:20,600
 Aboard my vessel, young fellow.

224
00:21:22,410 --> 00:21:25,750
 Did you find your way here?

225
00:21:26,010 --> 00:21:28,110
 You belong in a blood vessel, not in the kidney.

226
00:21:28,850 --> 00:21:31,570
 You see, I was having fun with my friends,

227
00:21:31,690 --> 00:21:34,130
 and all of a sudden I lost my grip and fell.

228
00:21:34,460 --> 00:21:38,270
 That's how I landed up in the suit.

229
00:21:39,510 --> 00:21:44,000
 I'm glad to hear that it was no more than an unfortunate accident,

230
00:21:44,160 --> 00:21:47,060
 because some kidney infections can give the urine a nasty red tint.

231
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:48,380
 However, we can relax now.

232
00:21:48,730 --> 00:21:53,040
 Please, may I go back to the bloodstream, sir, where I belong with all my friends?

233
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:55,160
 But of course you may, my young friend.

234
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:57,100
 We'll send you straight back with the next delivery.

235
00:21:57,240 --> 00:22:01,460
 You see, our job is to recover all the things that the body still needs.

236
00:22:02,570 --> 00:22:07,690
 Essential supplies like water, enzymes, salt and sugar are sent straight back into the bloodstream, you see,

237
00:22:07,770 --> 00:22:09,790
 while all the rubbish goes out with the urine.

238
00:22:10,190 --> 00:22:12,870
 I see. Is that what the clever people call recycling?

239
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:17,060
 That's the principle. Waste not, want not, I always say.

240
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:21,720
 And if the kidney fails to recycle all the materials it filters, they can block it.

241
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:23,110
 That must be all...

242
00:22:23,110 --> 00:22:30,020
 I recall in one of my previous existences, the kidney had been severely overloaded by alcohol abuse.

243
00:22:30,280 --> 00:22:33,480
 And it failed to get rid of all the waste products in the urine.

244
00:22:34,170 --> 00:22:35,480
 And what happened then?

245
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:38,270
 Things began to look bad.

246
00:22:40,400 --> 00:22:43,170
 Then things began to get worse.

247
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:07,250
 All the waste products built up and blocked the kidney.

248
00:23:07,410 --> 00:23:09,150
 It was absolutely excruciating.

249
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:10,050
 Then, Captain?

250
00:23:10,050 --> 00:23:13,050
 If the kidney stone stays in place, it's very dangerous.

251
00:23:13,190 --> 00:23:15,290
 But if it passes out, it's doubly excruciating.

252
00:23:15,690 --> 00:23:19,350
 It's awful.

253
00:23:19,780 --> 00:23:23,360
 It wasn't too bad. As soon as I gave up drinking, that is.

254
00:23:23,700 --> 00:23:27,180
 But that's all water under the bridge. How would you like to go on a tour of the ship?

255
00:23:27,430 --> 00:23:28,810
 I'd be pleased to show you round.

256
00:23:29,050 --> 00:23:29,630
 Thanks, Captain.

257
00:23:30,430 --> 00:23:44,380
 That water unloaded. Now for some salt, Eddie.

258
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,610
 Beg me your pardon, Mr. Mate. Any sugar to a float?

259
00:23:48,090 --> 00:23:49,390
 You are too late, sailor.

260
00:23:54,930 --> 00:24:00,140
 Children, I told you the kidney is efficient.

261
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:04,220
 And as I predicted earlier on, the blood components are now rejoining us.

262
00:24:04,540 --> 00:24:06,380
 Now doesn't that prove my point?

263
00:24:08,080 --> 00:24:13,150
 Look, all the unwanted waste products are poured away down the ureta.

264
00:24:15,970 --> 00:24:22,020
 So all the waste, anything useless or harmful to the body, flows down to the bladder.

265
00:24:22,290 --> 00:24:23,220
 And then after that?

266
00:24:23,630 --> 00:24:28,360
 Well, we shall see in just a moment because young Jumbo's bladder is full

267
00:24:28,360 --> 00:24:32,600
 and it's sending urgent signals he can no longer ignore.

268
00:24:33,160 --> 00:24:36,430
 So sorry, I had to.

269
00:24:36,430 --> 00:24:38,080
 And that's that.

